✅ What is SOLID?
SOLID is an acronym that stands for:

S – Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

O – Open/Closed Principle (OCP)

L – Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

I – Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

D – Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)



Let’s go one by one with Java examples.

🔹 1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
A class should have only one reason to change.

Each class should do only one job.

❌ Bad Example:


class UserService {
    public void registerUser() { }
    public void sendEmail() { } // Not the responsibility of UserService
}
✅ Good Example:

class UserService {
    public void registerUser() { }
}

class EmailService {
    public void sendEmail() { }
}



🔹 2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)
Software entities should be open for extension, but closed for modification.

You should be able to add new behavior without changing existing code.

✅ Example using interfaces:


interface Notification {
    void send(String message);
}

class EmailNotification implements Notification {
    public void send(String message) {
        System.out.println("Email: " + message);
    }
}

class SMSNotification implements Notification {
    public void send(String message) {
        System.out.println("SMS: " + message);
    }
}

class NotificationService {
    public void notifyUser(Notification notification, String message) {
        notification.send(message);
    }
}
➡ You can add PushNotification, SlackNotification, etc., without modifying NotificationService.




🔹 3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)
Subtypes must be substitutable for their base types.

A subclass should not break the behavior of the parent class.

❌ Bad Example:

class Bird {
    void fly() {}
}

class Ostrich extends Bird {
    void fly() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Ostrich can't fly!");
    }
}
✅ Good Example:


interface Bird { void eat(); }

interface FlyingBird extends Bird { void fly(); }

class Parrot implements FlyingBird {
    public void fly() { }
    public void eat() { }
}

class Ostrich implements Bird {
    public void eat() { }
}



🔹 4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
Don't force a class to implement more than it needs.

❌ Bad Example:

interface Worker {
    void work();
    void eat();
}

class Robot implements Worker {
    public void work() {}
    public void eat() {} // Robots don't eat!
}
✅ Good Example:


interface Workable {
    void work();
}

interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}

class Human implements Workable, Eatable {
    public void work() {}
    public void eat() {}
}

class Robot implements Workable {
    public void work() {}
}




🔹 5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)
Depend on abstractions, not on concrete implementations.

❌ Bad Example:

class MySQLDatabase {
    public void connect() {}
}

class Application {
    private MySQLDatabase db = new MySQLDatabase();
}
✅ Good Example:


interface Database {
    void connect();
}

class MySQLDatabase implements Database {
    public void connect() {}
}
class PostgresDatabase implements Database {
    public void connect() {}
}
// here if we found MULTIPLE classes that implements database
 then we use (@Primary (default choice) or @Qualifier (EXPLICIT choice) or @Profile (environment-based) )

class Application {
    private Database db;

    public Application(@Qualifier("mysqldatabase") Database db) {
        this.db = db;
    }
}
✅ Final takeaway
| Situation                | What Spring does |
| ------------------------ | ---------------- |
| One implementation       | Auto inject      |
| Multiple implementations | Error            |
| Use `@Primary`           | Default winner   |
| Use `@Qualifier`         | Explicit winner  |
| Use `@Profile`           | Env-based        |



➡ Now you can inject PostgresDatabase or OracleDatabase without changing Application.



🧠 Summary Table
| Principle | Meaning                                 |
| --------- | --------------------------------------- |
| **S**     | One class = One responsibility          |
| **O**     | Extend code without modifying it        |
| **L**     | Subclass should behave like parent      |
| **I**     | Keep interfaces focused                 |
| **D**     | Rely on interfaces, not implementations |


